Yes — this is exactly the state I would expect **before Cilium**.

Your control plane is healthy enough to proceed:

* all 3 `etcd-*` pods are `Running`
* all 3 `kube-apiserver-*` pods are `Running`
* all 3 controller managers and schedulers are `Running`
* all 3 nodes are registered
* etcd endpoint health is good
* the only node readiness blocker is still the missing CNI

The pending pods are also consistent with that. `CoreDNS`, metrics-server, ingress, runtimeclasses, and snapshot-controller jobs are waiting because the nodes are still `NotReady`.

So your next action should be:

```bash
ansible-playbook \
  -i inventory/dev/hosts.ini \
  playbooks/deploy-cilium.yml \
  --limit=masters
```

or whatever your actual Cilium playbook name is.

After Cilium deploys, check in this order:

```bash
ansible master01 \
  -i inventory/dev/hosts.ini \
  -b \
  -m shell \
  -a '/var/lib/rancher/rke2/bin/kubectl --kubeconfig /etc/rancher/rke2/rke2.yaml get pods -n kube-system -o wide | grep -i cilium'
```

Then:

```bash
ansible master01 \
  -i inventory/dev/hosts.ini \
  -b \
  -m shell \
  -a '/var/lib/rancher/rke2/bin/kubectl --kubeconfig /etc/rancher/rke2/rke2.yaml get nodes'
```

What you want to see is:

```text
master01   Ready
master02   Ready
master03   Ready
```

Then check CoreDNS:

```bash
ansible master01 \
  -i inventory/dev/hosts.ini \
  -b \
  -m shell \
  -a '/var/lib/rancher/rke2/bin/kubectl --kubeconfig /etc/rancher/rke2/rke2.yaml get pods -n kube-system -o wide | grep coredns'
```

Expected transition:

```text
Pending
  ↓
ContainerCreating
  ↓
Running
```

I would also check whether your Cilium configuration is intended to replace kube-proxy or coexist with it. Right now `kube-proxy` is already running on all three masters. If your Cilium values use:

```yaml
kubeProxyReplacement: true
```

then you should normally disable kube-proxy in the RKE2 config before Cilium deployment.

If instead your Cilium deployment is configured with kube-proxy replacement disabled, then the current state is fine.

That is the one setting I would verify before executing the Cilium playbook.
